{% if comparison_rule.noise_declared %}
`better` and `worse` mean the difference exceeds {{ '%g' | format(comparison_rule.verdict_noises) }} noises in the metric's direction;
`within noise` means this measurement cannot tell the candidate from its base.
{% else %}
This task declares no per-run noise, so the noise is estimated from the runs themselves:
there is no verdict until the candidate and its base each have at least
{{ comparison_rule.min_runs }} runs, and their runs do not all read alike. Until then the result is
`inconclusive`: the ratio is not a gain and it is not noise, it is a number from too few runs
to say either. Then `better` and `worse` mean the difference exceeds a critical number of
noises (Welch's t test at 95%), larger the fewer runs there are; `within noise` means the
runs held show a spread the difference does not exceed. While a result is inconclusive or
within noise, the framework runs the candidate and its base again itself, up to
{{ comparison_rule.min_runs }} runs each. If every run on both sides reads alike, no verdict is
possible and no more runs are taken.
{% endif %}
Evaluating unchanged candidate content again starts no new run (only one the machinery
